module bindings.ruby.request_key_percent_decode_after_authorization;

// A request path can be named in more than one spelling: percent-encoded
// (`/node/foo%2520bar`), decoded once (`/node/foo%20bar`), and decoded twice
// (`/node/foo bar`). Whichever spelling reaches an authorization decision is
// the one the policy was written against, and any decoding performed after
// that decision names a resource the policy never saw.
//
// The Ruby standard library documents the decoders: `URI.unescape` (and
// `decode_www_form_component`, its modern spelling) and `CGI.unescape` turn
// escape sequences into the characters they stand for, Rack ships
// `Rack::Utils.unescape` for the same job, and Addressable names its decoder
// `unencode`. The authorization
// hooks are the conventional ones -- Pundit's `authorize`, CanCanCan's
// `authorize!`, the framework hooks `check_authorization`, `action_auth_level`,
// `check_ownership`, `require_owner`, `owns_resource?` and `can?` -- the same
// vocabulary the Ruby `core.AuthorizationCheck` bindings already treat as an
// access decision.
//
// So a function that assigns a percent-decoded value while an authorization
// hook runs in it has the two spellings in one place: the hook matched the
// pre-decode string and whatever the function returns or looks up carries the
// post-decode one. Fix 6592a816 for puppet's `IndirectedRoutes#
// uri2indirection` is the canonical case: it authorized
// `check_authorization(method, "#{url_prefix}/#{indirection_name}/#{key}",
// params)` and only then ran `key = URI.unescape(key)` before returning the
// key that drives `indirection.find`. The fix removes the decode from the
// function and performs it once at the HTTP edge instead, so the decode
// conjunct stops holding however the remediation is spelled -- the decode
// leaves the function, or the authorization does.
//
// The two conjuncts deliberately take different routes:
//
//   * the decode is read from the function's own `assign:` tokens
//     (`key = URI.unescape(key)` emits `assign:key=URI.unescape(key)`), which
//     are matched against the function context's own facts and nothing else;
//   * the authorization hook is a call fact, and in Ruby a positive call
//     conjunct on a function context cannot be confined today: the context
//     node carries no lexical scope, and a scope-call miss falls back to the
//     file-wide call search, so any hook anywhere in the file satisfies it.
//     Requiring the decode assignment first keeps the reported function one
//     that decodes; the residual is a sibling method's hook crediting a
//     function that decodes and never authorizes.
//
// The tokens are AST-derived, so a comment naming either side cannot stand in
// for the call or the assignment.
//
// Further costs, stated rather than hidden. The context token set carries no
// order, so a function that decodes first and authorizes the decoded value
// afterwards -- the correct inline arrangement -- reports as well; the
// edge-decode arrangement the puppet fix uses is the one that stops
// reporting. A decode nested inside another call (`find(URI.unescape(k))`) or
// returned bare emits no `assign:` token and is not reported. And the two
// facts combine by co-occurrence, so a function that decodes one value and
// authorizes an unrelated one reports too. On the reachable set -- every
// non-test Ruby file across the corpus clones -- a percent-decode assignment
// and an authorization hook co-occur in one file only in the code that
// motivated this binding.
binding requestKeyPercentDecodeAfterAuthorization {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "ruby"
    and containsAny(node.context.assign, ["=URI.unescape(", "=CGI.unescape(", "=Rack.Utils.unescape(", "=unencode(", "=decode_www_form_component(", "=unescape("])
    and node.scopeCall.method in ["authorize", "authorize!", "can?", "check_authorization", "action_auth_level", "check_ownership", "require_owner", "owns_resource?"]
  emit issue code.MiddlewarePathMatchNormalizationMismatch at node
}
